window.LEXY_IELTS={"reading":[{"id":"forgetting","title":"Why we forget","format":"academic","minutes":20,"topic":"Психология","passage":{"heading":"Why we forget","paragraphs":["For most of the twentieth century, forgetting was treated as a failure: a slow leak in an otherwise reliable container. The German psychologist Hermann Ebbinghaus, who spent years memorising lists of meaningless syllables in the 1880s, produced the first 'forgetting curve', showing that much of what we learn disappears within a day unless it is reviewed. His conclusion seemed simple. Memories fade with time, and the only defence is repetition.","Recent research paints a more interesting picture. Neuroscientists now argue that the brain forgets actively, and that this process is not a defect but a feature. Studies on fruit flies and mice have identified specific molecules that weaken connections between neurons, deliberately removing information that has not been used. When these molecules are blocked, the animals remember more, but they also become worse at adapting when their environment changes.","The reason, researchers suggest, is that memory did not evolve to store the past for its own sake. Its purpose is to help an organism make good decisions in the future. A mouse that perfectly remembers where food was found last month, but cannot let go of that information when the food source disappears, will waste time and energy. Forgetting allows the brain to replace outdated details with more useful general rules.","This view helps to explain a puzzling observation about human learners. People who are tested on material, rather than simply rereading it, remember considerably more a week later, even though the test feels harder at the time. Psychologists call this the 'testing effect'. One explanation is that the act of retrieving a fading memory signals to the brain that the information is still needed, so the memory is strengthened rather than cleared away.","The timing of practice matters as well. In a well-known series of experiments, students who spread their study sessions over several days outperformed those who studied for the same total time in a single block. The advantage was largest when the gap between sessions was long enough for some forgetting to begin. Cramming, it appears, produces knowledge that is easy to access the next morning but disappears rapidly afterwards.","Sleep plays a further role. During deep sleep, the brain replays patterns of activity from the previous day, and memories that were tagged as important appear to be transferred from short-term storage in the hippocampus to more permanent networks in the cortex. Not every researcher agrees on how this transfer works, and some recent studies suggest that dreaming sleep, rather than deep sleep, is responsible for linking new information to existing knowledge.","For teachers and learners, the practical message is fairly clear. Short, repeated tests are more effective than long reading sessions; reviews should be spaced out rather than squeezed together; and a good night's sleep before an exam is likely to be worth more than an extra hour of late-night revision. Forgetting cannot be avoided, but it can be put to work."]},"groups":[{"title":"Questions 1-5","instructions":"Do the following statements agree with the information in the passage? Write TRUE, FALSE or NOT GIVEN.","questions":[{"id":"q1","kind":"tfng","prompt":"Ebbinghaus used real words from everyday German in his experiments.","answer":["FALSE"],"why":"В тексте: «meaningless syllables», то есть бессмысленные слоги, а не настоящие слова."},{"id":"q2","kind":"tfng","prompt":"Ebbinghaus found that repetition was the only way to prevent memories from fading.","answer":["TRUE"],"why":"«the only defence is repetition» в первом абзаце."},{"id":"q3","kind":"tfng","prompt":"Animals whose forgetting molecules were blocked found it harder to adapt to change.","answer":["TRUE"],"why":"«they also become worse at adapting when their environment changes»."},{"id":"q4","kind":"tfng","prompt":"The testing effect was first described by Ebbinghaus.","answer":["NOT GIVEN"],"why":"Кто первым описал testing effect, в тексте не сказано."},{"id":"q5","kind":"tfng","prompt":"Students usually find tests easier than rereading.","answer":["FALSE"],"why":"«even though the test feels harder at the time», то есть тест кажется труднее."}]},{"title":"Questions 6-9","instructions":"Choose the correct letter, A, B, C or D.","questions":[{"id":"q6","kind":"mcq","prompt":"According to the passage, the main purpose of memory is to","options":["A  keep an accurate record of past events.","B  help an organism make better future decisions.","C  store as much information as possible.","D  protect the brain from stress."],"answer":["B"],"why":"«Its purpose is to help an organism make good decisions in the future»."},{"id":"q7","kind":"mcq","prompt":"The example of the mouse is used to show that","options":["A  animals remember food better than humans.","B  perfect memory can be a disadvantage.","C  mice forget food sources quickly.","D  memory improves with hunger."],"answer":["B"],"why":"Мышь, которая не может забыть старое место еды, тратит время и силы."},{"id":"q8","kind":"mcq","prompt":"In the spaced-practice experiments, the benefit was greatest when","options":["A  sessions were very short.","B  students studied in a single block.","C  some forgetting had started between sessions.","D  students slept less than usual."],"answer":["C"],"why":"«largest when the gap between sessions was long enough for some forgetting to begin»."},{"id":"q9","kind":"mcq","prompt":"What does the writer say about the role of sleep?","options":["A  Scientists fully agree on how it works.","B  Only deep sleep affects memory.","C  There is disagreement about which stage of sleep matters most.","D  Dreaming prevents new learning."],"answer":["C"],"why":"«Not every researcher agrees…» и часть исследований говорит про dreaming sleep."}]},{"title":"Questions 10-13","instructions":"Complete the sentences. Choose ONE WORD ONLY from the passage for each answer.","questions":[{"id":"q10","kind":"gap","prompt":"Forgetting helps the brain swap old details for more useful general ______.","answer":["rules"],"why":"«replace outdated details with more useful general rules»."},{"id":"q11","kind":"gap","prompt":"Retrieving a fading memory tells the brain the information is still ______.","answer":["needed"],"why":"«signals to the brain that the information is still needed»."},{"id":"q12","kind":"gap","prompt":"Knowledge gained through ______ is easy to recall the next day but is soon lost.","answer":["cramming"],"why":"«Cramming, it appears, produces knowledge that is easy to access the next morning…»."},{"id":"q13","kind":"gap","prompt":"During deep sleep, important memories move from the hippocampus to the ______.","answer":["cortex"],"why":"«to more permanent networks in the cortex»."}]}]},{"id":"coworking","title":"Notice: Riverside co-working space","format":"general","minutes":15,"topic":"Работа и быт","passage":{"heading":"Riverside Hub: information for new members","paragraphs":["Welcome to Riverside Hub. The building is open to members from 7 am to 10 pm on weekdays and from 9 am to 6 pm at weekends. Members on the Night Owl plan may also use the ground floor between 10 pm and 2 am, using the side entrance on Mill Lane. The main doors are locked automatically at 10 pm.","Hot desks are available on the first and second floors on a first-come, first-served basis. Please do not leave personal belongings on a desk for longer than one hour if you are not using it. Members who need the same desk every day can upgrade to the Fixed Desk plan, which also includes a lockable drawer.","Meeting rooms can be booked through the members' app up to two weeks in advance. Each member receives four free hours of meeting-room time per month; additional hours are charged at £12 per hour. Bookings that are cancelled less than 24 hours in advance will still be deducted from your free hours.","Tea and coffee are free throughout the day. The kitchen on the second floor has a fridge, which is cleared every Friday at 5 pm. Anything left inside will be thrown away, so please label your food with your name and the date.","Printing is available on every floor. The first 50 black-and-white pages each month are included in your membership; colour printing costs 20p per page. To print, send your document to print@riversidehub.co.uk from the email address you registered with.","Guests are welcome but must be signed in at reception, and members are responsible for their guests at all times. Each member may bring up to two guests per day without charge. Guests are not allowed to use the building outside staffed hours, which are 8 am to 6 pm."]},"groups":[{"title":"Questions 1-6","instructions":"Do the following statements agree with the information in the passage? Write TRUE, FALSE or NOT GIVEN.","questions":[{"id":"g1","kind":"tfng","prompt":"All members can enter the building at midnight on weekdays.","answer":["FALSE"],"why":"Ночью можно только по тарифу Night Owl."},{"id":"g2","kind":"tfng","prompt":"Night Owl members must use a different entrance after 10 pm.","answer":["TRUE"],"why":"«using the side entrance on Mill Lane»."},{"id":"g3","kind":"tfng","prompt":"The Fixed Desk plan costs more than the standard plan.","answer":["NOT GIVEN"],"why":"Цены тарифов в тексте не указаны."},{"id":"g4","kind":"tfng","prompt":"A booking cancelled 12 hours before the meeting is still counted.","answer":["TRUE"],"why":"Отмена позже чем за 24 часа всё равно списывается."},{"id":"g5","kind":"tfng","prompt":"The kitchen fridge is emptied twice a week.","answer":["FALSE"],"why":"Только по пятницам в 17:00."},{"id":"g6","kind":"tfng","prompt":"Guests may stay in the building until 10 pm if a member is with them.","answer":["FALSE"],"why":"Гости только в часы работы персонала, с 8 до 18."}]},{"title":"Questions 7-10","instructions":"Answer the questions. Write NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER from the text for each answer.","questions":[{"id":"g7","kind":"gap","prompt":"How many free hours of meeting-room time does a member get each month?","answer":["four","4","four hours","4 hours"],"why":"«four free hours of meeting-room time per month»."},{"id":"g8","kind":"gap","prompt":"What should members write on their food, as well as their name?","answer":["the date","date"],"why":"«label your food with your name and the date»."},{"id":"g9","kind":"gap","prompt":"How much does one page of colour printing cost?","answer":["20p","20 p","20 pence"],"why":"«colour printing costs 20p per page»."},{"id":"g10","kind":"gap","prompt":"What do Fixed Desk members get in addition to their own desk?","answer":["lockable drawer","a lockable drawer","drawer"],"why":"«also includes a lockable drawer»."}]}]},{"id":"urban-bees","title":"The urban bee","format":"academic","minutes":20,"topic":"Экология","passage":{"heading":"The quiet comeback of the urban bee","paragraphs":["For decades, cities were regarded as hostile territory for bees. Concrete replaced meadows, gardens were paved over for parking, and the few flowering plants that remained were often treated with pesticides. It was widely assumed that if bees were to survive anywhere, it would be in the countryside, far from traffic and pollution.","That assumption has been turned on its head. A growing body of research suggests that many cities now support a greater variety of bee species than the farmland around them. A survey in the English city of Bristol, for instance, found more species of wild bee in residential gardens than in nearby fields, and similar results have been reported in Berlin, Melbourne and several cities in North America.","The explanation lies partly in what has happened to the countryside. Modern agriculture favours enormous fields planted with a single crop. When that crop flowers, bees enjoy a brief feast, but for the rest of the year there is very little to eat. Hedges, which once provided food and shelter, have been removed on a large scale, and farm chemicals have reduced the number of wild flowers growing at the edges of fields.","Cities, by contrast, offer what ecologists call a continuous supply of flowers. Because gardeners plant species from all over the world, something is almost always in bloom, from early spring bulbs to late-flowering plants in autumn. Parks, railway embankments and even cemeteries add to this mosaic, and many local councils have stopped mowing roadside verges so frequently, allowing wild flowers to return.","Not every bee benefits equally. Species that nest in the ground need patches of bare, undisturbed soil, which are scarce in tidy urban gardens. Specialist bees, which feed on only one or two kinds of plant, also struggle unless their particular plants happen to grow nearby. The winners tend to be generalists, which will visit almost any flower, and species that nest in hollow stems or small holes in walls.","The popularity of urban beekeeping has created a further complication. Rooftop hives have become fashionable, and in some cities the number of honeybee colonies has risen sharply in recent years. Honeybees are excellent pollinators, but a single hive may contain tens of thousands of workers, and researchers in Paris have warned that in areas with too many hives, wild bees may be left with too little food.","For this reason, most conservation groups now argue that the best way for city dwellers to help bees is not to keep them, but to feed them. Planting a variety of flowers, leaving a corner of the garden untidy and avoiding pesticides can all make a measurable difference. The urban bee, it seems, does not need a beekeeper so much as a careless gardener."]},"groups":[{"title":"Questions 1-6","instructions":"Do the following statements agree with the information in the passage? Write TRUE, FALSE or NOT GIVEN.","questions":[{"id":"ub1","kind":"tfng","prompt":"In the past, scientists believed that bees were safer in the countryside than in cities.","answer":["TRUE"],"why":"«It was widely assumed that if bees were to survive anywhere, it would be in the countryside»."},{"id":"ub2","kind":"tfng","prompt":"The Bristol survey found more wild bee species in gardens than in nearby fields.","answer":["TRUE"],"why":"«found more species of wild bee in residential gardens than in nearby fields»."},{"id":"ub3","kind":"tfng","prompt":"Large single-crop fields provide food for bees throughout the year.","answer":["FALSE"],"why":"Еда только во время цветения: «for the rest of the year there is very little to eat»."},{"id":"ub4","kind":"tfng","prompt":"Cemeteries are among the best places in a city for bees.","answer":["NOT GIVEN"],"why":"Кладбища названы частью «мозаики», но не лучшим местом."},{"id":"ub5","kind":"tfng","prompt":"Bees that nest in the ground usually do well in urban gardens.","answer":["FALSE"],"why":"Им нужна голая земля, «which are scarce in tidy urban gardens»."},{"id":"ub6","kind":"tfng","prompt":"The number of honeybee colonies has increased in some cities.","answer":["TRUE"],"why":"«the number of honeybee colonies has risen sharply in recent years»."}]},{"title":"Questions 7-9","instructions":"Choose the correct letter, A, B, C or D.","questions":[{"id":"ub7","kind":"mcq","prompt":"According to the writer, cities offer bees","options":["A  fewer pesticides than farms.","B  flowers at almost every time of year.","C  more hedges than the countryside.","D  larger areas of a single plant."],"answer":["B"],"why":"«continuous supply of flowers… something is almost always in bloom»."},{"id":"ub8","kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which bees are most likely to succeed in cities?","options":["A  bees that feed on one type of plant","B  bees that nest in bare soil","C  bees that visit many kinds of flower","D  bees that live in large colonies"],"answer":["C"],"why":"«The winners tend to be generalists, which will visit almost any flower»."},{"id":"ub9","kind":"mcq","prompt":"What concern have researchers in Paris raised?","options":["A  Rooftop hives are dangerous for people.","B  Honeybees are poor pollinators.","C  Too many hives may reduce food for wild bees.","D  Urban honey is of low quality."],"answer":["C"],"why":"«in areas with too many hives, wild bees may be left with too little food»."}]},{"title":"Questions 10-13","instructions":"Complete the summary. Choose ONE WORD ONLY from the passage for each answer.","questions":[{"id":"ub10","kind":"gap","prompt":"On farms, many ______ that once gave bees food and shelter have been removed.","answer":["hedges"],"why":"«Hedges, which once provided food and shelter, have been removed»."},{"id":"ub11","kind":"gap","prompt":"Some councils now mow roadside ______ less often.","answer":["verges"],"why":"«stopped mowing roadside verges so frequently»."},{"id":"ub12","kind":"gap","prompt":"Some urban bees nest in hollow ______.","answer":["stems"],"why":"«species that nest in hollow stems»."},{"id":"ub13","kind":"gap","prompt":"The writer suggests that bees need a careless ______ more than a beekeeper.","answer":["gardener"],"why":"Последняя фраза: «a careless gardener»."}]}]},{"id":"light-at-night","title":"Light at night","format":"academic","minutes":20,"topic":"Наука","passage":{"heading":"Losing the dark","paragraphs":["A century ago, almost everyone on Earth could look up on a clear night and see the Milky Way. Today, according to a widely cited study published in 2016, around a third of humanity, and four out of five people in Europe and North America, can no longer see it from where they live. The cause is artificial light, which now brightens the night sky over most of the inhabited world.","Light pollution was first treated as a serious problem by astronomers, whose telescopes were losing their view of faint objects. Observatories were moved to remote mountains, and a few towns near them introduced rules on street lighting. For a long time, however, the issue was seen as a concern for a small group of specialists rather than for society as a whole.","That view began to change when biologists started to document the effects of artificial light on wildlife. Newly hatched sea turtles, which find the ocean by moving towards the brightest horizon, are now regularly drawn towards hotels and roads instead. Migrating birds, which use the stars to navigate, may circle lit buildings until they are exhausted. Insects are perhaps the worst affected: a single streetlight can attract and kill large numbers of moths in one night, and some researchers believe this is one of the causes of the recent decline in insect populations.","Humans are not immune either. Our bodies keep time with a daily rhythm that is set largely by light. In the evening, darkness triggers the release of melatonin, a hormone that helps us fall asleep. Exposure to bright light, particularly the blue-rich light of modern screens and LED lamps, delays this process. Although the long-term health consequences are still debated, there is good evidence that light in the evening makes it harder to fall asleep and reduces the quality of sleep.","Ironically, the switch to energy-efficient LED street lamps has in some places made the problem worse. Because LEDs are cheap to run, many cities have installed more lights, or brighter ones, than before. Satellite images show that the area of the planet lit at night grew by around two per cent a year between 2012 and 2016.","Yet light pollution is unusual among environmental problems in one important respect: it can be reversed almost instantly. When a light is switched off, the pollution disappears. Simple measures, such as shielding lamps so that they shine only downwards, using warmer colours and dimming lights after midnight, can greatly reduce the glow without making streets less safe. Several regions have now created official dark-sky reserves, where such rules are applied and where, once again, the Milky Way can be seen."]},"groups":[{"title":"Questions 1-5","instructions":"Do the following statements agree with the claims of the writer? Write YES, NO or NOT GIVEN.","questions":[{"id":"ln1","kind":"ynng","prompt":"Most people in North America can no longer see the Milky Way from their homes.","answer":["YES"],"why":"«four out of five people in Europe and North America, can no longer see it»."},{"id":"ln2","kind":"ynng","prompt":"Light pollution was considered a problem for everyone from the very beginning.","answer":["NO"],"why":"Долго его считали проблемой «a small group of specialists»."},{"id":"ln3","kind":"ynng","prompt":"Artificial light is the main reason for the decline in insect numbers.","answer":["NO"],"why":"Автор говорит осторожнее: «one of the causes», а не главная причина."},{"id":"ln4","kind":"ynng","prompt":"Blue-rich light delays the release of melatonin.","answer":["YES"],"why":"«Exposure to bright light, particularly the blue-rich light… delays this process»."},{"id":"ln5","kind":"ynng","prompt":"LED street lamps are more expensive to run than older lamps.","answer":["NO"],"why":"«Because LEDs are cheap to run»."}]},{"title":"Questions 6-9","instructions":"Choose the correct letter, A, B, C or D.","questions":[{"id":"ln6","kind":"mcq","prompt":"How did astronomers first respond to light pollution?","options":["A  by moving observatories to remote places","B  by asking cities to switch off all lights","C  by building larger telescopes","D  by studying its effects on animals"],"answer":["A"],"why":"«Observatories were moved to remote mountains»."},{"id":"ln7","kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why are young sea turtles affected by artificial light?","options":["A  It makes them too hot.","B  They move towards the brightest horizon.","C  It hides the moon from them.","D  It attracts their predators."],"answer":["B"],"why":"Черепахи ищут океан по самому яркому горизонту, и их тянет к отелям."},{"id":"ln8","kind":"mcq","prompt":"What does the writer find ironic about LED lamps?","options":["A  They are less efficient than expected.","B  They are harmful to astronomers.","C  Their low cost has led to more lighting.","D  They cannot be dimmed."],"answer":["C"],"why":"Дешевизна привела к тому, что ставят больше и ярче фонарей."},{"id":"ln9","kind":"mcq","prompt":"According to the last paragraph, light pollution is unusual because","options":["A  it affects only cities.","B  it can be stopped very quickly.","C  it is invisible from space.","D  it is cheap to measure."],"answer":["B"],"why":"«it can be reversed almost instantly»."}]},{"title":"Questions 10-13","instructions":"Complete the sentences. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS from the passage for each answer.","questions":[{"id":"ln10","kind":"gap","prompt":"Migrating birds may fly around lit buildings until they become ______.","answer":["exhausted"],"why":"«may circle lit buildings until they are exhausted»."},{"id":"ln11","kind":"gap","prompt":"Melatonin is a ______ that helps people fall asleep.","answer":["hormone"],"why":"«melatonin, a hormone that helps us fall asleep»."},{"id":"ln12","kind":"gap","prompt":"Lamps can be ______ so that their light goes only downwards.","answer":["shielded"],"why":"«shielding lamps so that they shine only downwards»."},{"id":"ln13","kind":"gap","prompt":"In official ______, rules on lighting are applied.","answer":["dark-sky reserves","dark sky reserves"],"why":"«official dark-sky reserves, where such rules are applied»."}]}]},{"id":"vanishing-languages","title":"How languages disappear","format":"academic","minutes":20,"topic":"Общество","passage":{"heading":"The silence of languages","paragraphs":["Linguists estimate that around seven thousand languages are spoken in the world today. The distribution of speakers, however, is extremely uneven. Fewer than a hundred languages are used by the great majority of the world's population, while thousands of others have only a few hundred or a few thousand speakers. Many of these smaller languages are considered endangered, and some experts predict that half of them could disappear by the end of this century.","A language rarely dies because all its speakers die suddenly. Far more often, the process is gradual and takes place over three generations. Parents who speak a minority language decide, often for practical reasons, to raise their children in a more widely spoken language that offers better access to education and jobs. The children may understand their parents' language but do not use it themselves, and their own children grow up without it altogether.","This shift is not always the result of pressure from outside. In many cases it reflects decisions made within families who want their children to succeed. Yet researchers point out that these decisions are shaped by wider forces: schools that teach only in the national language, the spread of television and the internet in a handful of global languages, and the movement of young people from villages to cities.","Why should the loss of a language matter? One argument is scientific. Each language represents a unique solution to the problem of how to organise sounds, words and grammar, and linguists can learn a great deal about the human mind from this variety. Some languages, for example, have no words for left and right, and describe positions only in terms of compass directions. Another argument concerns knowledge. Speakers of small languages often have detailed vocabularies for local plants, animals and weather, and much of this knowledge has never been written down.","Not all attempts to save endangered languages succeed, but there are notable examples of revival. Hebrew, which for centuries was used mainly in religious texts, became the everyday language of millions of people in the twentieth century. In New Zealand, 'language nests', pre-school centres where young children are surrounded by the Maori language, helped to create a new generation of speakers. Welsh, once in steady decline, is now taught in every school in Wales.","Technology, often blamed for the decline of small languages, may also help them. Speakers of endangered languages can now record stories, publish dictionaries online and communicate with each other through social media. On its own, however, technology cannot save a language. As one linguist puts it, a language survives only if children learn it at home and find a reason to use it with their friends."]},"groups":[{"title":"Questions 1-5","instructions":"Do the following statements agree with the information in the passage? Write TRUE, FALSE or NOT GIVEN.","questions":[{"id":"vl1","kind":"tfng","prompt":"Most of the world's population speaks fewer than a hundred languages.","answer":["TRUE"],"why":"«Fewer than a hundred languages are used by the great majority»."},{"id":"vl2","kind":"tfng","prompt":"Languages usually disappear because all their speakers die at the same time.","answer":["FALSE"],"why":"«A language rarely dies because all its speakers die suddenly»."},{"id":"vl3","kind":"tfng","prompt":"Children in the second generation often understand their parents' language but do not speak it.","answer":["TRUE"],"why":"«The children may understand… but do not use it themselves»."},{"id":"vl4","kind":"tfng","prompt":"Governments usually force families to stop using minority languages.","answer":["NOT GIVEN"],"why":"Говорится о школах и СМИ, но не о том, что правительства обычно заставляют семьи."},{"id":"vl5","kind":"tfng","prompt":"Welsh is now taught in all schools in Wales.","answer":["TRUE"],"why":"«now taught in every school in Wales»."}]},{"title":"Questions 6-9","instructions":"Choose the correct letter, A, B, C or D.","questions":[{"id":"vl6","kind":"mcq","prompt":"According to paragraph 2, parents switch to a more widely spoken language mainly","options":["A  because they have forgotten their own language.","B  for practical reasons such as jobs and education.","C  because the law requires it.","D  to communicate with grandparents."],"answer":["B"],"why":"«often for practical reasons… better access to education and jobs»."},{"id":"vl7","kind":"mcq","prompt":"The example of languages without words for left and right is used to show","options":["A  how simple some languages are.","B  why such languages are disappearing.","C  the variety of ways languages work.","D  how difficult translation can be."],"answer":["C"],"why":"Пример иллюстрирует уникальность языков и разнообразие решений."},{"id":"vl8","kind":"mcq","prompt":"What were 'language nests' in New Zealand?","options":["A  online dictionaries","B  pre-school centres using Maori","C  university courses","D  television programmes"],"answer":["B"],"why":"«pre-school centres where young children are surrounded by the Maori language»."},{"id":"vl9","kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the writer's view of technology?","options":["A  It is the main cause of language loss.","B  It can save any language on its own.","C  It can help, but it is not enough by itself.","D  It is too expensive for small communities."],"answer":["C"],"why":"«On its own, however, technology cannot save a language»."}]},{"title":"Questions 10-13","instructions":"Complete the summary. Choose ONE WORD ONLY from the passage for each answer.","questions":[{"id":"vl10","kind":"gap","prompt":"Language loss usually happens over three ______.","answer":["generations"],"why":"«takes place over three generations»."},{"id":"vl11","kind":"gap","prompt":"Young people moving from villages to ______ contribute to the shift.","answer":["cities"],"why":"«the movement of young people from villages to cities»."},{"id":"vl12","kind":"gap","prompt":"Hebrew was used mainly in ______ texts for centuries.","answer":["religious"],"why":"«used mainly in religious texts»."},{"id":"vl13","kind":"gap","prompt":"A language survives only if children learn it at ______.","answer":["home"],"why":"«only if children learn it at home»."}]}]},{"id":"library-guide","title":"Library membership guide","format":"general","minutes":15,"topic":"Быт","passage":{"heading":"Westbridge Public Library: a guide for new members","paragraphs":["Membership of Westbridge Public Library is free for anyone who lives, works or studies in the borough. To join, bring one document with your current address, such as a utility bill or bank statement, to any of our four branches. Children under sixteen can join with the signature of a parent or guardian.","Adult members may borrow up to twelve items at a time, including books, audiobooks and DVDs. Books can be kept for three weeks and DVDs for one week. Loans can be renewed twice, online, by phone or in person, unless another member has reserved the item. There are no fines for late books for children, but adults are charged 25p per day for each overdue book, up to a maximum of £5 per item.","If the book you want is out on loan, you can reserve it through the online catalogue. Reservations are free for children and cost 50p for adults. When the item is ready, we will send you a text message or an email, and it will be kept for you at the branch of your choice for seven days.","Every branch has free Wi-Fi and public computers. Computers can be booked for one hour at a time, and members can book a maximum of two hours per day. Printing costs 10p per page in black and white and 40p per page in colour. Scanning is free.","Our Central Library on Market Street has a quiet study room on the second floor, which is open to members from 9 am until 8 pm. Group study rooms are also available there, but they must be booked at least 24 hours in advance.","The library also runs a range of free events, including weekly story time for under-fives every Tuesday morning, a monthly book club and computer classes for beginners. Most events are free, but places on computer classes are limited and should be reserved at the information desk."]},"groups":[{"title":"Questions 1-6","instructions":"Do the following statements agree with the information in the passage? Write TRUE, FALSE or NOT GIVEN.","questions":[{"id":"lg1","kind":"tfng","prompt":"People who work in the borough can join the library even if they live elsewhere.","answer":["TRUE"],"why":"«free for anyone who lives, works or studies in the borough»."},{"id":"lg2","kind":"tfng","prompt":"New members need to bring two documents with their address.","answer":["FALSE"],"why":"«bring one document with your current address»."},{"id":"lg3","kind":"tfng","prompt":"DVDs can be borrowed for three weeks.","answer":["FALSE"],"why":"Книги на три недели, DVD на одну."},{"id":"lg4","kind":"tfng","prompt":"Children do not pay fines for books returned late.","answer":["TRUE"],"why":"«There are no fines for late books for children»."},{"id":"lg5","kind":"tfng","prompt":"The library has more computers than any other library in the region.","answer":["NOT GIVEN"],"why":"Сравнения с другими библиотеками в тексте нет."},{"id":"lg6","kind":"tfng","prompt":"Scanning documents is free of charge.","answer":["TRUE"],"why":"«Scanning is free»."}]},{"title":"Questions 7-10","instructions":"Answer the questions. Write NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER for each answer.","questions":[{"id":"lg7","kind":"gap","prompt":"What is the maximum fine for one overdue book?","answer":["£5","5","5 pounds","five pounds"],"why":"«up to a maximum of £5 per item»."},{"id":"lg8","kind":"gap","prompt":"How many days is a reserved item kept at the branch?","answer":["7","seven","seven days","7 days"],"why":"«it will be kept for you… for seven days»."},{"id":"lg9","kind":"gap","prompt":"On which floor of the Central Library is the quiet study room?","answer":["second","the second","2nd","second floor"],"why":"«a quiet study room on the second floor»."},{"id":"lg10","kind":"gap","prompt":"On which day does story time for under-fives take place?","answer":["tuesday","tuesdays","tuesday morning"],"why":"«every Tuesday morning»."}]}]},{"id":"museum-jobs","title":"Summer jobs at the museum","format":"general","minutes":15,"topic":"Работа","passage":{"heading":"Coastal Heritage Museum: summer vacancies","paragraphs":["The Coastal Heritage Museum is looking for friendly, reliable people to join our team for the summer season, which runs from 1 June to 15 September. No previous museum experience is needed, as full training will be provided during the last week of May.","Visitor Assistants welcome visitors, answer questions and help keep the galleries safe and tidy. Applicants must be at least eighteen and comfortable standing for long periods. Assistants work five days a week, including at least one weekend day, and are paid £11.40 per hour.","Café Staff prepare drinks and light meals and serve customers in our seafront café. A food hygiene certificate is an advantage but not essential, because successful applicants without one will be trained. Café staff work early or late shifts and receive a free lunch on every working day.","Family Activity Leaders run craft sessions and guided trails for children aged five to eleven. This role suits people who have experience of working with children, for example as a teacher, coach or camp leader. Activity leaders must have a background check, which the museum will arrange and pay for.","All seasonal staff receive free entry to the museum for two guests per visit, a 20% discount in the museum shop and free parking in the staff car park. Uniforms are provided, but staff should wear their own comfortable black shoes.","To apply, complete the online form on our website and attach a short covering letter explaining which role interests you and why. Please do not send a CV by post. The closing date for applications is 30 April, and interviews will take place in the first two weeks of May."]},"groups":[{"title":"Questions 1-5","instructions":"Which role does each statement describe? Write VA (Visitor Assistant), CS (Café Staff) or FL (Family Activity Leader).","questions":[{"id":"mj1","kind":"mcq","prompt":"Staff in this role get a free meal at work.","options":["VA","CS","FL"],"answer":["CS"],"why":"У Café Staff «a free lunch on every working day»."},{"id":"mj2","kind":"mcq","prompt":"This role requires a check that the museum pays for.","options":["VA","CS","FL"],"answer":["FL"],"why":"Activity Leaders «must have a background check, which the museum will arrange and pay for»."},{"id":"mj3","kind":"mcq","prompt":"People in this role must work at least one day at the weekend.","options":["VA","CS","FL"],"answer":["VA"],"why":"Visitor Assistants «including at least one weekend day»."},{"id":"mj4","kind":"mcq","prompt":"A certificate would be useful but is not required.","options":["VA","CS","FL"],"answer":["CS"],"why":"Для кафе «a food hygiene certificate is an advantage but not essential»."},{"id":"mj5","kind":"mcq","prompt":"Experience with children is helpful for this role.","options":["VA","CS","FL"],"answer":["FL"],"why":"«suits people who have experience of working with children»."}]},{"title":"Questions 6-10","instructions":"Complete the sentences. Write NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER from the text for each answer.","questions":[{"id":"mj6","kind":"gap","prompt":"Training takes place during the last week of ______.","answer":["may"],"why":"«during the last week of May»."},{"id":"mj7","kind":"gap","prompt":"Visitor Assistants earn £______ per hour.","answer":["11.40","11,40"],"why":"«paid £11.40 per hour»."},{"id":"mj8","kind":"gap","prompt":"Seasonal staff get a ______ discount in the shop.","answer":["20%","20 %","20 percent","20 per cent"],"why":"«a 20% discount in the museum shop»."},{"id":"mj9","kind":"gap","prompt":"Staff must provide their own black ______.","answer":["shoes","comfortable shoes"],"why":"«wear their own comfortable black shoes»."},{"id":"mj10","kind":"gap","prompt":"Applications must be sent by ______.","answer":["30 april","april 30","30th april","the 30th of april"],"why":"«The closing date for applications is 30 April». И не по почте: только онлайн-форма."}]}]}],"listening":[{"id":"sports-centre","title":"Joining a sports centre","part":1,"context":"A man phones a sports centre to ask about membership.","speakers":{"A":{"name":"Receptionist","voice":"female"},"B":{"name":"Caller","voice":"male"}},"script":[{"speaker":"A","text":"Good morning, Westfield Sports Centre, Hannah speaking. How can I help?"},{"speaker":"B","text":"Hi. I'm thinking of joining, and I'd like to know a bit about the memberships you offer."},{"speaker":"A","text":"Of course. We have three options at the moment. The basic membership is twenty-five pounds a month and gives you the gym and the pool."},{"speaker":"B","text":"Right. And what about classes? I'm quite interested in yoga."},{"speaker":"A","text":"Classes come with the Plus membership. That one used to be thirty-five pounds, but from this month it's thirty-nine pounds a month."},{"speaker":"B","text":"Thirty-nine. OK. Is there a joining fee?"},{"speaker":"A","text":"Normally there's a fee of twenty pounds, but if you join before the end of October we don't charge it."},{"speaker":"B","text":"Great. And when is the centre open?"},{"speaker":"A","text":"On weekdays we open at six thirty in the morning and close at ten in the evening. At weekends it's eight until eight."},{"speaker":"B","text":"Do I need to book the yoga classes in advance?"},{"speaker":"A","text":"Yes, through our app. The most popular one is on Wednesday evenings, so it's worth booking early. Thursday's class is usually quieter."},{"speaker":"B","text":"OK. Could I sign up over the phone?"},{"speaker":"A","text":"Certainly. Can I have your full name, please?"},{"speaker":"B","text":"Yes, it's Daniel Kowalski. That's K, O, W, A, L, S, K, I."},{"speaker":"A","text":"Thank you. And your address?"},{"speaker":"B","text":"Forty-seven Harbour Road. That's in Eastbridge."},{"speaker":"A","text":"Forty-seven Harbour Road. And a contact number?"},{"speaker":"B","text":"It's oh seven nine three one, double four eight, two six one."},{"speaker":"A","text":"Lovely. One last thing: we ask all new members to bring some photo ID on their first visit, a passport or a driving licence is fine."},{"speaker":"B","text":"I'll bring my driving licence. Do I need anything else?"},{"speaker":"A","text":"Just a padlock for the lockers. We do sell them at reception for four pounds if you forget."},{"speaker":"B","text":"Perfect. Thanks very much for your help."}],"groups":[{"title":"Questions 1-10","instructions":"Complete the form. Write ONE WORD AND/OR A NUMBER for each answer.","questions":[{"id":"l1","kind":"gap","prompt":"Basic membership: £______ per month","answer":["25","twenty-five","twenty five"],"why":"«The basic membership is twenty-five pounds a month»."},{"id":"l2","kind":"gap","prompt":"Basic membership includes the gym and the ______","answer":["pool"],"why":"«gives you the gym and the pool»."},{"id":"l3","kind":"gap","prompt":"Plus membership: £______ per month","answer":["39","thirty-nine","thirty nine"],"why":"Ловушка: было 35, теперь 39."},{"id":"l4","kind":"gap","prompt":"No joining fee if you join before the end of ______","answer":["october"],"why":"«if you join before the end of October»."},{"id":"l5","kind":"gap","prompt":"Weekday opening time: ______ am","answer":["6.30","6:30","630","six thirty"],"why":"«we open at six thirty in the morning»."},{"id":"l6","kind":"gap","prompt":"Most popular yoga class: ______ evening","answer":["wednesday"],"why":"Ловушка: четверг назван, но он «quieter»."},{"id":"l7","kind":"gap","prompt":"Surname: ______","answer":["kowalski"],"why":"Фамилию продиктовали по буквам: K-O-W-A-L-S-K-I."},{"id":"l8","kind":"gap","prompt":"Address: 47 ______ Road, Eastbridge","answer":["harbour","harbor"],"why":"«Forty-seven Harbour Road»."},{"id":"l9","kind":"gap","prompt":"Phone: 07931 ______","answer":["448261","448 261"],"why":"«double four eight, two six one» = 448261."},{"id":"l10","kind":"gap","prompt":"Bring a ______ for the lockers","answer":["padlock"],"why":"«Just a padlock for the lockers»."}]}]},{"id":"river-festival","title":"The Riverside Festival","part":2,"context":"A local radio presenter gives information about a weekend festival.","speakers":{"A":{"name":"Presenter","voice":"female"},"B":{"name":"Presenter","voice":"female"}},"script":[{"speaker":"A","text":"Good afternoon, and welcome to What's On. Today I want to tell you about the Riverside Festival, which returns to Millbrook this weekend for the twelfth time."},{"speaker":"A","text":"The festival was first held in a small car park behind the town hall, but these days it takes over the whole of Victoria Park, right next to the river."},{"speaker":"A","text":"It runs from Friday evening until Sunday, and entry is free, although some workshops have a small charge. The organisers expect around fifteen thousand visitors, which is about three thousand more than last year."},{"speaker":"A","text":"Let's start with the main stage. On Friday night the opening concert begins at seven, not at eight as it says in the printed programme. That change was made because of the fireworks, which will now start at half past nine."},{"speaker":"A","text":"On Saturday, families should head to the area called the Green Corner. There you'll find face painting, a puppet theatre and, new this year, a climbing wall run by the local scout group."},{"speaker":"A","text":"If you're interested in food, the market will have more than forty stalls. Most of them are run by local farms and bakeries. This year the organisers have asked every stall to use plates and cups that can be composted, so please don't bring disposable plastic."},{"speaker":"A","text":"Now, a few practical points. Parking in the town centre will be very limited, so you're strongly advised to use the park-and-ride. Buses leave from the sports stadium every fifteen minutes, and a return ticket costs two pounds fifty. Children under twelve travel free."},{"speaker":"A","text":"If you want to join a workshop, for example the pottery class or the drumming session, you need to book in advance. You can't book by phone this year. Everything has to be done through the festival website, and places are limited to twenty people per session."},{"speaker":"A","text":"Finally, the organisers are still looking for volunteers. They especially need people to help at the information tent on Sunday morning. Volunteers receive a free T-shirt and a voucher for the food market."},{"speaker":"A","text":"So, that's the Riverside Festival. Let's hope the weather stays dry."}],"groups":[{"title":"Questions 1-5","instructions":"Choose the correct letter, A, B or C.","questions":[{"id":"rf1","kind":"mcq","prompt":"Where was the festival held for the first time?","options":["A  in Victoria Park","B  behind the town hall","C  next to the river"],"answer":["B"],"why":"«first held in a small car park behind the town hall». Victoria Park и река — это сейчас."},{"id":"rf2","kind":"mcq","prompt":"How many visitors are expected this year?","options":["A  about 3,000","B  about 12,000","C  about 15,000"],"answer":["C"],"why":"«around fifteen thousand visitors». 3 000 — это на сколько больше, чем в прошлом году."},{"id":"rf3","kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why has the start time of the opening concert changed?","options":["A  because of the fireworks","B  because of a printing mistake","C  because of the weather"],"answer":["A"],"why":"«That change was made because of the fireworks»."},{"id":"rf4","kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is new in the Green Corner this year?","options":["A  a puppet theatre","B  face painting","C  a climbing wall"],"answer":["C"],"why":"«new this year, a climbing wall». Остальное было и раньше."},{"id":"rf5","kind":"mcq","prompt":"What have the food stalls been asked to do?","options":["A  sell only local food","B  use compostable plates and cups","C  stop selling drinks"],"answer":["B"],"why":"«use plates and cups that can be composted»."}]},{"title":"Questions 6-10","instructions":"Complete the notes. Write ONE WORD AND/OR A NUMBER for each answer.","questions":[{"id":"rf6","kind":"gap","prompt":"Opening concert on Friday starts at ______ pm","answer":["7","seven","7pm","7 pm"],"why":"Ловушка: в программе 8, но концерт начнётся в семь."},{"id":"rf7","kind":"gap","prompt":"Park-and-ride buses leave from the sports ______","answer":["stadium"],"why":"«Buses leave from the sports stadium»."},{"id":"rf8","kind":"gap","prompt":"Return bus ticket: £______","answer":["2.50","2,50","2.5","two fifty"],"why":"«a return ticket costs two pounds fifty»."},{"id":"rf9","kind":"gap","prompt":"Workshops can only be booked on the festival ______","answer":["website"],"why":"«You can't book by phone… through the festival website»."},{"id":"rf10","kind":"gap","prompt":"Volunteers are needed at the information tent on Sunday ______","answer":["morning"],"why":"«help at the information tent on Sunday morning»."}]}]},{"id":"sleep-project","title":"A project on sleep and memory","part":3,"context":"Two students, Maya and Leon, discuss a research project for their psychology course.","speakers":{"A":{"name":"Maya","voice":"female2"},"B":{"name":"Leon","voice":"male"}},"script":[{"speaker":"A","text":"Leon, have you had a chance to look at the feedback on our project proposal?"},{"speaker":"B","text":"I have. Dr Harris liked the topic, sleep and memory, but she thinks our sample is too small. We said twenty students, and she wants at least forty."},{"speaker":"A","text":"Forty is a lot, but I think we can find them. The first-year students are always looking for ways to earn course credits."},{"speaker":"B","text":"True. The other thing she mentioned was the method. We planned to use a questionnaire about how much people sleep, but she says people are bad at estimating their own sleep."},{"speaker":"A","text":"So what does she suggest? Those sleep trackers?"},{"speaker":"B","text":"Not trackers, they're too expensive for forty people. She suggests a sleep diary. Each student writes down when they go to bed and when they wake up, every day for two weeks."},{"speaker":"A","text":"That sounds more reliable, but it's also more work for the participants. We'll need to remind them every day."},{"speaker":"B","text":"We could send a text message every evening. I can set that up."},{"speaker":"A","text":"Great. And for the memory part, are we still using the word list?"},{"speaker":"B","text":"Yes, but I'd like to change the timing. Instead of testing them the next morning, we test them two days later. Otherwise we only measure short-term recall."},{"speaker":"A","text":"That makes sense. What about analysing the results? I'm not very confident with statistics."},{"speaker":"B","text":"Neither am I, to be honest. But the library runs a statistics workshop on Thursdays. I think we should both go before we start collecting data."},{"speaker":"A","text":"Good idea. And let's divide the writing. I'm happy to do the literature review, because I've already read most of the articles."},{"speaker":"B","text":"Then I'll take the method section. We can write the discussion together at the end."},{"speaker":"A","text":"Perfect. When is the final report due? Still the end of May?"},{"speaker":"B","text":"No, they moved it. It's now due on the tenth of June, which gives us a bit more time."}],"groups":[{"title":"Questions 1-5","instructions":"Choose the correct letter, A, B or C.","questions":[{"id":"sp1","kind":"mcq","prompt":"What did Dr Harris say about the sample?","options":["A  It should include first-year students only.","B  It should be twice as large.","C  It should include older adults."],"answer":["B"],"why":"Было 20, нужно минимум 40, то есть в два раза больше."},{"id":"sp2","kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why does Dr Harris not like the questionnaire?","options":["A  It takes too long to complete.","B  It is too expensive.","C  People do not judge their own sleep accurately."],"answer":["C"],"why":"«people are bad at estimating their own sleep»."},{"id":"sp3","kind":"mcq","prompt":"What method will the students use to record sleep?","options":["A  sleep trackers","B  a sleep diary","C  interviews"],"answer":["B"],"why":"Трекеры дорогие, Dr Harris предлагает sleep diary."},{"id":"sp4","kind":"mcq","prompt":"How will the students remind participants?","options":["A  by email","B  by phone call","C  by text message"],"answer":["C"],"why":"«We could send a text message every evening»."},{"id":"sp5","kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why does Leon want to change the timing of the memory test?","options":["A  to measure longer-term memory","B  to make the test easier","C  because the room is not available"],"answer":["A"],"why":"Иначе «we only measure short-term recall»."}]},{"title":"Questions 6-10","instructions":"Complete the sentences. Write NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER for each answer.","questions":[{"id":"sp6","kind":"gap","prompt":"Participants will keep records for ______ weeks.","answer":["2","two"],"why":"«every day for two weeks»."},{"id":"sp7","kind":"gap","prompt":"Memory will be tested ______ days after learning the words.","answer":["2","two"],"why":"«we test them two days later»."},{"id":"sp8","kind":"gap","prompt":"The statistics workshop takes place on ______.","answer":["thursday","thursdays"],"why":"«the library runs a statistics workshop on Thursdays»."},{"id":"sp9","kind":"gap","prompt":"Maya will write the ______.","answer":["literature review"],"why":"«I'm happy to do the literature review»."},{"id":"sp10","kind":"gap","prompt":"The final report is due on ______ June.","answer":["10","10th","tenth","the tenth"],"why":"Ловушка: конец мая уже не актуален, теперь 10 июня."}]}]},{"id":"living-light","title":"Lecture: living light","part":4,"context":"A lecturer in marine biology talks about bioluminescence.","speakers":{"A":{"name":"Lecturer","voice":"male"},"B":{"name":"Lecturer","voice":"male"}},"script":[{"speaker":"A","text":"Today I'm going to talk about bioluminescence, that is, the production of light by living organisms. Most people have seen it only once or twice, perhaps as fireflies on a summer evening, but in the ocean it is extremely common."},{"speaker":"A","text":"In fact, researchers now estimate that around three quarters of the animals living in the open ocean can produce some kind of light. On land, by contrast, it is quite rare."},{"speaker":"A","text":"So how does it work? In almost every case, the light comes from a chemical reaction. A molecule called luciferin reacts with oxygen, with the help of an enzyme, and the energy is released as light rather than heat. That is why bioluminescence is sometimes described as cold light."},{"speaker":"A","text":"The colour of the light is interesting too. In the sea, most bioluminescence is blue, because blue light travels furthest through water. Red light, on the other hand, disappears very quickly, and as a result many deep-sea animals cannot see it at all."},{"speaker":"A","text":"One fish, called the dragonfish, takes advantage of this. It produces red light and uses it like a secret torch, so it can see its prey without being seen."},{"speaker":"A","text":"Why do animals produce light? Scientists usually mention three main purposes. The first is to find food. The anglerfish, for example, has a glowing lure above its mouth that attracts smaller fish."},{"speaker":"A","text":"The second purpose is defence. Some squid release a cloud of glowing liquid when they are attacked. The predator follows the light, and the squid escapes in the darkness."},{"speaker":"A","text":"The third purpose is communication, especially finding a partner. Fireflies are the best known example: each species flashes in its own pattern, a bit like a code."},{"speaker":"A","text":"Bioluminescence is also becoming useful to humans. In medical research, the genes responsible for light production are inserted into cells, so that scientists can watch how a disease spreads. And some engineers are even experimenting with glowing trees as a form of street lighting, although that is still a long way from being practical."},{"speaker":"A","text":"In our next session, we'll look at how climate change is affecting the creatures that produce light."}],"groups":[{"title":"Questions 1-10","instructions":"Complete the notes. Write ONE WORD ONLY for each answer.","questions":[{"id":"ll1","kind":"gap","prompt":"Bioluminescence is common in the ocean but ______ on land.","answer":["rare"],"why":"«On land, by contrast, it is quite rare»."},{"id":"ll2","kind":"gap","prompt":"The light is produced when luciferin reacts with ______.","answer":["oxygen"],"why":"«reacts with oxygen, with the help of an enzyme»."},{"id":"ll3","kind":"gap","prompt":"It is called cold light because energy is not released as ______.","answer":["heat"],"why":"«released as light rather than heat»."},{"id":"ll4","kind":"gap","prompt":"Most light in the sea is ______, because this colour travels furthest.","answer":["blue"],"why":"«most bioluminescence is blue, because blue light travels furthest»."},{"id":"ll5","kind":"gap","prompt":"Many deep-sea animals cannot see ______ light.","answer":["red"],"why":"«many deep-sea animals cannot see it at all» про красный свет."},{"id":"ll6","kind":"gap","prompt":"The dragonfish uses its light like a ______.","answer":["torch"],"why":"«like a secret torch»."},{"id":"ll7","kind":"gap","prompt":"The anglerfish has a glowing ______ to attract fish.","answer":["lure"],"why":"«a glowing lure above its mouth»."},{"id":"ll8","kind":"gap","prompt":"Some squid escape by releasing glowing ______.","answer":["liquid"],"why":"«a cloud of glowing liquid»."},{"id":"ll9","kind":"gap","prompt":"Each species of firefly flashes in its own ______.","answer":["pattern"],"why":"«each species flashes in its own pattern»."},{"id":"ll10","kind":"gap","prompt":"Engineers are testing glowing trees as street ______.","answer":["lighting","lights"],"why":"«glowing trees as a form of street lighting»."}]}]}],"writing":[{"id":"w-uni-free","task":"task2","format":"both","type":"Opinion","minutes":40,"minWords":250,"text":"Some people think that university education should be free for all students. Others believe that students should pay for their own studies.\n\nDiscuss both views and give your own opinion.","model":"Whether higher education should be funded by the state or by students themselves is a question that divides many societies. While both positions have merit, I believe that university should be free, at least for students who could not otherwise afford it.\n\nThose who argue that students should pay point out that a degree brings considerable personal benefits. Graduates typically earn more over their lifetimes than people without a degree, so it seems reasonable that they, rather than taxpayers, should cover the cost. Fees may also encourage students to take their studies seriously and to choose courses that lead to employment.\n\nOn the other hand, free education ensures that talent, not family income, decides who goes to university. When fees are high, capable young people from low-income families are often discouraged from applying, which is both unfair and a waste of potential. Moreover, society as a whole gains from an educated population: doctors, engineers and teachers serve everyone, not only themselves, so it is logical for the public to share the cost of training them.\n\nIn my view, the second argument is more convincing. A country that charges high fees risks turning higher education into a privilege of the wealthy. However, free tuition need not mean unlimited spending. Governments could fund places in subjects the economy needs and ask graduates with high incomes to repay part of the cost later through the tax system.\n\nIn conclusion, although graduates do benefit personally from their degrees, I believe that the wider social advantages justify making university education free for students who need it."},{"id":"w-remote","task":"task2","format":"both","type":"Advantages & disadvantages","minutes":40,"minWords":250,"text":"More and more companies allow their employees to work from home.\n\nDo the advantages of this trend outweigh the disadvantages?","model":"In recent years, working from home has changed from a rare privilege into a normal arrangement for millions of employees. Although this trend has some drawbacks, I believe that its advantages are greater.\n\nThe most obvious benefit is the time and money saved on commuting. An employee who used to spend two hours a day in traffic can now use that time to rest, exercise or spend with family, which often improves both wellbeing and productivity. Companies also benefit, since they can reduce the cost of renting large offices and recruit talented staff who live far from the city centre.\n\nNevertheless, remote work is not without problems. Many people find it difficult to separate their work from their private life when both take place in the same room, and some end up working longer hours than before. In addition, young employees may miss the informal learning that happens when they sit next to experienced colleagues, and teams can find it harder to build trust without face-to-face contact.\n\nHowever, most of these disadvantages can be reduced through sensible management. Companies can set clear working hours, organise regular days when teams meet in person, and provide mentoring programmes for new staff. By contrast, the benefits of flexibility, such as reduced stress and access to a wider pool of workers, are difficult to achieve in any other way.\n\nIn conclusion, while working from home can lead to isolation and blurred boundaries between work and leisure, these problems can be managed, so the advantages of the trend clearly outweigh its disadvantages."},{"id":"w-cities","task":"task2","format":"both","type":"Problem & solution","minutes":40,"minWords":250,"text":"In many cities, the cost of housing has risen much faster than salaries.\n\nWhat problems does this cause, and what can governments do to solve them?","model":"In many major cities, rents and property prices have grown far more quickly than wages, putting a home out of reach for a large part of the population. This situation creates serious problems, but governments have several tools to address it.\n\nThe most immediate consequence is financial pressure on ordinary households. When a family spends half of its income on rent, little is left for savings, education or emergencies, and many young adults are forced to delay starting a family. High housing costs also push key workers, such as nurses and teachers, to live far from the city centre, which leads to long commutes and staff shortages in essential services. In the long term, cities may become places where only the wealthy can afford to live, which weakens the social mix that makes urban life vibrant.\n\nTo solve these problems, governments should first increase the supply of housing. Simplifying planning rules and releasing unused public land for construction would allow more homes to be built, which would slow the rise in prices. Secondly, authorities can invest directly in social or affordable housing with rents linked to local incomes, as cities such as Vienna have done successfully for decades. Finally, measures such as taxing empty properties can discourage investors from buying homes simply to leave them vacant.\n\nIn conclusion, the gap between housing costs and salaries places a heavy burden on families and on public services. A combination of building more homes, funding affordable housing and discouraging speculation offers the most realistic way to make cities liveable for everyone again."},{"id":"w-phones","task":"task2","format":"both","type":"Agree / disagree","minutes":40,"minWords":250,"text":"Children under the age of fourteen should not be allowed to own a smartphone.\n\nTo what extent do you agree or disagree?","model":"Smartphones have become an ordinary part of childhood in many countries, and some people argue that children under fourteen should not be allowed to own one. While I accept that smartphones carry real risks for young children, I only partly agree with a complete ban.\n\nThere are strong reasons to be cautious. Many apps are designed to be addictive, and children are less able than adults to control the time they spend on them. Excessive screen time has been linked to poor sleep and reduced concentration at school. Furthermore, a smartphone gives a child unsupervised access to social media, where they may experience bullying or see content that is not suitable for their age.\n\nHowever, a strict ban ignores the practical benefits of these devices. Parents often want their children to carry a phone so that they can be contacted on the way to and from school, especially in large cities. Smartphones can also be valuable learning tools, giving access to dictionaries, educational videos and language-learning apps. In addition, a child who has never used a smartphone may struggle to develop the digital skills that modern life requires.\n\nFor these reasons, I believe that rules are more effective than prohibition. Young children could be given simple phones without internet access, while older children could use smartphones with parental controls, time limits and no social media accounts. Schools can also play a role by teaching pupils how to use technology responsibly.\n\nIn conclusion, although smartphones can harm young children if used without limits, I disagree that they should be banned completely for everyone under fourteen. Sensible restrictions and guidance offer a better balance between safety and opportunity."},{"id":"w-tourism","task":"task2","format":"both","type":"Two-part question","minutes":40,"minWords":250,"text":"International tourism has grown rapidly in recent decades, and some popular destinations now receive more visitors than they can cope with.\n\nWhy is this happening? What can be done to reduce the negative effects?","model":"Over the past few decades, the number of international tourists has increased dramatically, and places such as Venice, Barcelona and Bali now struggle with overcrowding. Several factors explain this growth, and a number of measures could reduce its harmful effects.\n\nThe main reason is that travel has become far cheaper and easier. Low-cost airlines have reduced the price of flights, while booking websites allow anyone to arrange accommodation in minutes. Rising incomes in large countries such as China and India have also created millions of new travellers. In addition, social media encourages people to visit the same famous locations, since photographs of them are widely shared, so tourists tend to concentrate in a small number of places.\n\nThis concentration causes a range of problems. Local residents may be forced out of city centres as homes are converted into short-term rentals, and historic sites can be damaged by the sheer number of visitors. Overcrowding also spoils the experience for tourists themselves.\n\nThere are, however, practical ways to manage the situation. Popular destinations can introduce tourist taxes and use the income to maintain monuments and public services. Daily limits on visitors, as already applied at some national parks, can protect fragile sites. Cities can also regulate short-term rentals to protect housing for local people. Finally, governments and travel companies could promote less famous regions, which would spread visitors more evenly and bring income to areas that need it.\n\nIn conclusion, cheaper travel and the influence of social media have led to overcrowding in popular destinations, but taxes, visitor limits and better promotion of alternative places can significantly reduce the negative impact."},{"id":"w-online-learning","task":"task2","format":"both","type":"Opinion","minutes":40,"minWords":250,"text":"Some people believe that online courses will soon replace traditional classroom teaching.\n\nTo what extent do you agree or disagree?","model":"Online learning has expanded enormously in recent years, and some people predict that it will soon make traditional classrooms unnecessary. Although I agree that online courses will play a growing role in education, I do not believe they will replace face-to-face teaching.\n\nOnline courses certainly have impressive advantages. They allow students to learn at their own pace and from any location, which is particularly valuable for adults who work or for people living far from good schools. They are often cheaper than traditional courses, and the best lecturers can reach millions of learners rather than a few dozen. For motivated students, this flexibility can be extremely effective.\n\nNevertheless, classroom teaching offers things that are difficult to reproduce online. Many learners, especially children, need the structure of a timetable and the presence of a teacher to stay focused. In a classroom, teachers can notice immediately when a student is confused and adapt their explanation. Moreover, schools are not only places of instruction; they are where young people develop social skills, learn to cooperate and form friendships. These aspects of education cannot easily be delivered through a screen.\n\nIt is therefore more likely that the two forms will be combined. Many universities already use a blended approach, in which students watch recorded lectures at home and use class time for discussion and practical work. This model makes use of the strengths of both methods.\n\nIn conclusion, while online courses will continue to grow and change the way people study, I disagree that they will replace traditional classrooms, which provide support, motivation and social development that technology alone cannot offer."},{"id":"w-internet-age","task":"task1","format":"academic","type":"Line graph","minutes":20,"minWords":150,"text":"The graph below shows the percentage of people in one country who used the internet every day, by age group, between 2008 and 2023.\n\nSummarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","chart":{"kind":"line","title":"Daily internet users by age group, 2008-2023 (%)","unit":"%","x":["2008","2011","2014","2017","2020","2023"],"series":[{"name":"16-24","values":[62,74,85,92,96,97]},{"name":"25-44","values":[48,61,74,83,90,93]},{"name":"45-64","values":[27,36,49,61,74,81]},{"name":"65+","values":[8,12,19,28,41,52]}]},"model":"The line graph illustrates the proportion of people in four age groups who went online every day in one country from 2008 to 2023.\n\nOverall, daily internet use rose steadily in every age group over the period. Young people were the most frequent users throughout, while the oldest group, although still the lowest, saw the most dramatic relative increase.\n\nIn 2008, 62% of 16- to 24-year-olds used the internet daily, compared with just under half of those aged 25 to 44. Both figures climbed consistently, reaching 97% and 93% respectively by 2023, which means that almost everyone in these age groups had become a daily user.\n\nThe middle-aged and older groups started from much lower levels. Only 27% of people aged 45 to 64 were online every day in 2008, but this figure tripled to 81% by the end of the period. The most striking change was among the over-65s: their daily use rose from a mere 8% to 52%, so by 2023 more than half of older people were going online every day. Notably, the growth in this group accelerated after 2017, when it gained 24 percentage points in six years.\n\nAs a result, the gap between the youngest and oldest users narrowed from 54 percentage points in 2008 to 45 in 2023, although it remained considerable."},{"id":"w-spending-bar","task":"task1","format":"academic","type":"Bar chart","minutes":20,"minWords":150,"text":"The chart below shows how an average household in one country spent its weekly budget in 1995 and 2020.\n\nSummarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","chart":{"kind":"bar","title":"Share of weekly household spending, 1995 and 2020 (%)","unit":"%","x":["Housing","Food","Transport","Leisure","Clothing"],"series":[{"name":"1995","values":[22,24,15,11,9]},{"name":"2020","values":[31,16,14,15,5]}]},"model":"The bar chart compares the proportion of an average household's weekly budget that was spent on five categories in 1995 and 2020.\n\nOverall, housing became by far the largest expense over the 25-year period, while the share spent on food and clothing fell considerably. Spending on leisure grew, whereas the proportion devoted to transport changed very little.\n\nIn 1995, food accounted for the largest share of household spending, at 24%, slightly ahead of housing at 22%. By 2020, however, the situation had reversed sharply: housing costs had risen to 31% of the budget, almost twice the share spent on food, which had dropped to 16%.\n\nAmong the smaller categories, leisure was the only one to increase, rising from 11% to 15%, so that in 2020 households spent nearly as much on entertainment and hobbies as on food. Transport remained relatively stable, falling by just one percentage point to 14%. Clothing, meanwhile, saw the steepest relative decline, with its share shrinking from 9% to only 5%.\n\nIn summary, the period saw a clear shift in household priorities, with a growing part of the budget going on housing and leisure, and a smaller part on basic goods such as food and clothes."},{"id":"w-energy-pie","task":"task1","format":"academic","type":"Pie charts","minutes":20,"minWords":150,"text":"The pie charts below show the sources of electricity in one country in 2005 and 2022.\n\nSummarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","chart":{"kind":"pie","title":"Sources of electricity, 2005 and 2022","unit":"%","pies":[{"title":"2005","slices":[{"label":"Coal","value":46},{"label":"Gas","value":30},{"label":"Nuclear","value":18},{"label":"Renewables","value":6}]},{"title":"2022","slices":[{"label":"Coal","value":9},{"label":"Gas","value":38},{"label":"Nuclear","value":15},{"label":"Renewables","value":38}]}]},"model":"The pie charts compare the proportion of electricity generated from four different sources in one country in 2005 and 2022.\n\nOverall, the country's electricity supply changed dramatically over the period. Coal, which had been the dominant source, almost disappeared, while renewable energy expanded enormously to become one of the two largest sources, alongside gas.\n\nIn 2005, coal produced nearly half of the country's electricity, at 46%. Gas was the second most important source, with 30%, followed by nuclear power at 18%. Renewable sources, such as wind and solar, contributed only a small share of 6%.\n\nBy 2022, the picture was very different. The share of coal had collapsed to just 9%, roughly a fifth of its earlier level. In contrast, renewables grew more than sixfold to 38%, equal to the share of gas, which had itself increased by eight percentage points. Nuclear energy was the most stable source, declining only slightly from 18% to 15%.\n\nIn short, over these 17 years the country moved away from coal towards gas and, in particular, renewable energy, which together supplied more than three quarters of its electricity in 2022."},{"id":"w-museums-table","task":"task1","format":"academic","type":"Table","minutes":20,"minWords":150,"text":"The table below shows the number of visitors to four museums in one city in 2015, 2019 and 2023.\n\nSummarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","chart":{"kind":"table","title":"Visitors to four city museums (thousands)","columns":["Museum","2015","2019","2023"],"rows":[["History Museum",820,910,760],["Science Centre",540,780,990],["Art Gallery",610,640,630],["Maritime Museum",230,210,150]]},"model":"The table gives information about the number of people who visited four museums in a city in 2015, 2019 and 2023.\n\nOverall, the Science Centre experienced the strongest growth and became the most popular museum by 2023, while the Maritime Museum, which was the least visited throughout, saw a steady decline. Attendance at the Art Gallery remained almost unchanged.\n\nIn 2015, the History Museum was the city's most visited attraction, with 820,000 visitors, and its numbers rose to 910,000 in 2019. By 2023, however, its attendance had fallen to 760,000, the lowest figure for this museum in the period.\n\nThe Science Centre followed a very different pattern. Its visitor numbers increased continuously, from 540,000 in 2015 to 780,000 in 2019 and then to 990,000 in 2023, almost doubling over eight years and overtaking the History Museum.\n\nThe Art Gallery attracted a stable audience of between 610,000 and 640,000 visitors in all three years. By contrast, the Maritime Museum, already the smallest, lost visitors in each period, dropping from 230,000 to only 150,000, a fall of roughly a third."},{"id":"w-letter-neighbour","task":"task1","format":"general","type":"Letter","minutes":20,"minWords":150,"text":"Your neighbour has been making a lot of noise late at night.\n\nWrite a letter to your neighbour. In your letter:\n• describe the problem\n• explain how it is affecting you\n• suggest what could be done\n\nBegin your letter: Dear …,","model":"Dear Mr Harrison,\n\nI hope you are well. I am writing to you about something that has been bothering me for the past few weeks, and I hope we can find a solution together.\n\nSince the beginning of the month, there has often been loud music and conversation coming from your flat late at night, sometimes until two or three in the morning. The noise is particularly noticeable from Thursday to Saturday, and our bedroom is directly next to your living room, so we can hear almost everything.\n\nUnfortunately, this is starting to affect my health and my work. I have to get up at six for my job at the hospital, and after several nights of poor sleep I find it very hard to concentrate. My young daughter has also been waking up during the night.\n\nI completely understand that you enjoy having friends over, and I would not want to stop that. Would it be possible to keep the volume down after eleven on weekdays, or perhaps to let me know in advance when you are planning a party? Placing the speakers away from our shared wall might also help a great deal.\n\nThank you for your understanding. I would be happy to talk about this in person if you prefer.\n\nBest regards,\nAnna Kowalczyk"},{"id":"w-letter-course","task":"task1","format":"general","type":"Letter","minutes":20,"minWords":150,"text":"You recently attended a short evening course at a local college, but you were not satisfied with it.\n\nWrite a letter to the college director. In your letter:\n• give details of the course\n• explain what the problems were\n• say what you would like the college to do\n\nBegin your letter: Dear Sir or Madam,","model":"Dear Sir or Madam,\n\nI am writing to express my dissatisfaction with the evening course in digital photography that I attended at your college from 3 March to 28 April. The course consisted of eight weekly sessions and cost £240.\n\nAccording to the brochure, the course was designed for beginners and promised practical sessions in the college studio. Unfortunately, the reality was quite different. Firstly, the studio was unavailable for six of the eight weeks, so most classes were held in an ordinary classroom with no lighting equipment. Secondly, the original tutor left after the second session, and the replacement teacher focused almost entirely on advanced editing software, which most of the group had never used before. As a result, several students stopped attending.\n\nI was particularly disappointed because I had chosen this course specifically for the hands-on experience it advertised, and I had arranged my work schedule around it.\n\nI would therefore like the college to refund at least half of the course fee. Alternatively, I would accept a free place on the next beginners' course, provided that the practical sessions take place as described.\n\nI look forward to hearing from you.\n\nYours faithfully,\nDaniel Moreau"}],"speaking":[{"id":"s1-home","part":1,"topic":"Home","prepSeconds":0,"answerSeconds":40,"questions":["Do you live in a house or a flat?","What do you like most about the place where you live?","Is there anything you would like to change about your home?","Do you plan to live there for a long time?"]},{"id":"s1-music","part":1,"topic":"Music","prepSeconds":0,"answerSeconds":40,"questions":["What kind of music do you enjoy?","Did you learn to play a musical instrument as a child?","Do you prefer listening to music alone or with friends?","Has your taste in music changed over the years?"]},{"id":"s1-phone","part":1,"topic":"Mobile phones","prepSeconds":0,"answerSeconds":40,"questions":["How often do you use your phone?","What do you mostly use it for?","Could you live without your phone for a week?","Do you think people spend too much time on their phones?"]},{"id":"s2-skill","part":2,"topic":"A skill","prepSeconds":60,"answerSeconds":120,"questions":["Describe a skill you learned that you are proud of.\n\nYou should say:\n• what the skill is\n• when and how you learned it\n• how difficult it was\nand explain why you are proud of it."]},{"id":"s2-place","part":2,"topic":"A place","prepSeconds":60,"answerSeconds":120,"questions":["Describe a place in your city where you like to spend time.\n\nYou should say:\n• where it is\n• how often you go there\n• what you do there\nand explain why you like this place."]},{"id":"s2-person","part":2,"topic":"A person","prepSeconds":60,"answerSeconds":120,"questions":["Describe a teacher who has influenced you.\n\nYou should say:\n• who this teacher was\n• what subject they taught\n• what they were like\nand explain how they influenced you."]},{"id":"s3-education","part":3,"topic":"Education","prepSeconds":0,"answerSeconds":60,"questions":["Why do some people find it easier to learn new skills than others?","Should schools focus more on practical skills than on academic subjects?","How might technology change the way people learn in the future?"]},{"id":"s3-cities","part":3,"topic":"Cities","prepSeconds":0,"answerSeconds":60,"questions":["What makes a city a good place to live?","Why do so many young people move from villages to cities?","What can governments do to reduce traffic in big cities?"]}]};
